    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d459">

                <pb facs="0391" n="379" sameAs="frus:frus1942v04/pg379"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.20/569: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Liberia</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Wharton</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-05-23T11:00:00-00:44">May 23, 1942—11
                            a.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 1:20 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>161. In view of Liberian Government’s invitation to Germans to leave country as
                    reported in my 159, May 22,<anchor n="26" xml:id="d459-f1"/> increased submarine
                    activity along Liberian coast is anticipated. To combat such activity, as well
                    as for other defense purposes, I urgently request that initial flight of three
                    light bombers be stationed here as soon as practicable. In this connection I
                    refer to last paragraph of Colonel McBride’s telegram 56, March 6. Such a
                    detachment would also go far at this time to reassure Liberian Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wharton</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
